### minimal-frustration
- Bryngelson and Wolynes, "Spin glasses and the statistical mechanics of protein folding" (*PNAS* 84:7524,
  1987), applied spin-glass theory to a simple model of folding and calculated its phase diagram; the
  principle that came out of it is minimal frustration — naturally evolved sequences have optimised energy
  landscapes, chosen so that the folded state is favoured. The review literature on frustration states the
  contrast: in physical systems frustration produces dynamics spanning many orders of magnitude in time, and
  some spin glasses never settle at all; biomolecules are the case nature evolved to have minimal
  frustration. Shakhnovich and Gutin (*Nature* 346:773, 1990) used the same methods for the probability that
  a randomly synthesised chain has a dominant fold at its global free-energy minimum.
- Berger and Leighton, "Protein folding in the hydrophobic-hydrophilic (HP) model is NP-complete"
  (*J. Comput. Biol.* 5(1):27–40, 1998; RECOMB 1998), prove NP-completeness for Dill's HP model — the chain a
  self-avoiding walk on the 3D cubic lattice, an optimum maximising adjacencies between hydrophobic
  residues; Crescenzi et al. proved the 2D case, and Hart and Istrail give a 3/8-approximation in 3D and a
  1/4-approximation in 2D.
- *Claude's gloss:* NP-completeness is a statement about the hardest instances of a toy model. A natural
  protein is one instance, from a distribution selected for funnelled landscapes and observed to fold in
  milliseconds to seconds, so the worst case does not bear on it; the folding time does, and it is measured.
  The inference runs from the observation — the same chain folds consistently — to the structure, a
  minimally frustrated landscape, and not from a theory to a hope.
