### coevolution-couplings
- Morcos, Pagnani, Lunt, Bertolino, Marks, Sander, Zecchina, Onuchic, Hwa and Weigt, "Direct-coupling
  analysis of residue coevolution captures native contacts across many protein families" (*PNAS*, 2011):
  DCA yields a large number of correctly predicted contacts and recapitulates the global structure of the
  contact map for most domains examined; what makes it work is disentangling direct from indirect
  correlations; and it captures signals from alternative conformations, ligand-mediated couplings and
  interdomain interactions in oligomers.
- Marks, Colwell, Sheridan, Hopf, Pagnani, Zecchina and Sander, "Protein 3D structure computed from
  evolutionary sequence variation" (*PLoS ONE* 6:e28766, 2011): a maximum-entropy model constrained by the
  alignment's statistics; the inferred coupling strength is an excellent predictor of proximity in the folded
  structure, the top couplings define the fold, and the signal gives 2.7–4.8 Å Cα-RMSD to the observed
  structure over at least two-thirds of the protein (EVfold).
- *Claude's gloss:* each coupling is a `Report` — value "i and j are in contact", warrant the alignment
  statistics and the model that separated direct from indirect correlation — and a predicted contact map is a
  `Derived` quantity with the alignment as its dependency, so `der-retracted` states what the field cannot: a
  contaminated alignment (a paralog mixed in, a fragment misaligned) withdraws every contact derived from it
  and every structure built from those contacts. And Morcos's finding that DCA sees alternative conformations
  means a coevolution map from a metamorphic family is the union of two contact maps, the fiber's two basins
  written over each other; reading it as one structure's contacts is the collapse `structure-predictor-carrier`
  records. The couplings report onto the `Taut` of basins, and a set of contacts no single compact fold can
  satisfy is the mark of a fiber with more than one basin.
